1. [Maximum mark: 19]
All of the relations in this question are defined on $\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}$.
(a) Decide, giving a proof or a counter-example, whether $xRy \Leftrightarrow x+y>7$ is
(i) reflexive;
(ii) symmetric;
(iii) transitive. [4 marks]
(b) Decide, giving a proof or a counter-example, whether $xRy \Leftrightarrow -2<x-y<2$ is
(i) reflexive;
(ii) symmetric;
(iii) transitive. [4 marks]
(c) Decide, giving a proof or a counter-example, whether $xRy \Leftrightarrow xy>0$ is
(i) reflexive;
(ii) symmetric;
(iii) transitive. [4 marks]
(d) Decide, giving a proof or a counter-example, whether $xRy \Leftrightarrow \frac{x}{y}\in\mathbb{Z}$ is
(i) reflexive;
(ii) symmetric;
(iii) transitive. [4 marks]
(e) One of the relations from parts (a), (b), (c) and (d) is an equivalence relation. For this relation, state what the equivalence classes are. [3 marks]
